  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCurrently the low stratus and patchy fog continue to hang on\nacross the state. In higher elevations there are still\nvisibilities between half and one mile. The trends the last few\nhours are the stratus breaking up, especially in the central.\nTonight with the moist southerly winds, more patchy fog is \npossible in the Highway 83 corridor, especially near rivers and \nbodies of water. \n\nAloft a ridge is over the Dakotas, with an open wave dry clipper\ndeveloping over the Canadian Rockies. Through the end of this \nweek temperatures will be near or above normal with the warmer \nair in the ridge. Late Friday night that clipper system will \npass by with a trailing front in a surface trough. This will \ncause winds across the state to be very breezy with west winds \nat 20 to 40 mph from the front and pressure gradient. Sunday \nwinds switch to the south as a high pressure in Colorado crates \na surface ridge over the Dakotas. With these strong southerly \nwinds, expect highs in the 60s. Temperatures will then stay \nabove normal (45 degrees) in the 50s through most of next week \nwith warmer flow from the Pacific Northwest. After Tuesday \nanother possible clipper system could bring some light rain or \nsnow in the far northeast and cooling highs to the 40s up there.\nElsewhere temperatures could remain 10 degrees above normal.",
